What to read next
Most of these are free to read online.
Henry David Thoreau, Walden (1854), the chapter “Economy.” The
original argument that the cost of a thing is the life you trade for it,
worked out against the price of a house. Free at Project Gutenberg
Oscar Wilde, The Soul of Man under Socialism (1891). A short, funny,
serious essay about letting machines do the necessary work so people
can do the rest. Free at Project Gutenberg
Peter Kropotkin, Fields, Factories and Workshops (1899, revised 1913).
The case for putting the factory amidst the fields, and for regions that
can feed and supply themselves. Free at Project Gutenberg
John Maynard Keynes, “Economic Possibilities for our
Grandchildren” (1930). A famous economist predicts the end of the
economic problem, and worries about what weʼll do next. Free to read
Aneurin Bevan, In Place of Fear (1952). The man who launched the
NHS explains why care should never depend on the means to pay. 
